Yep. I’m making this one an adventure-comedy mystery where the ingredients actually become causal instead of just being tossed into a blender.

Nicolas Cage knew almost nothing about American football.

This had never prevented Nicolas Cage from having opinions about American football.

“The forward pass,” he once told a confused bartender, “was the moment football stopped being territorial warfare and became correspondence.”

Nobody knew what that meant.

Including Cage.

But he liked it.

Which is how he ended up in the Congo Basin carrying a football beside Scooby-Doo.

There was context.

Not enough.

But some.

Three days earlier, Cage had been hired to narrate a wildlife documentary called Giants of the River.

His responsibilities were straightforward:

stand near a waterfall,

look contemplative,

and say things like:

“Here, beneath the ancient canopy, the river remembers.”

Cage considered this beneath his abilities.

Then they told him there were hippos.

He changed his mind immediately.

“Hippos,” Cage explained to the producer, “are proof that God occasionally designs animals by committee.”

The producer wrote nothing down.

Now Cage stood beside a tributary somewhere in the Congo Basin as thousands of gallons of water thundered over a broad rocky shelf into a misty gorge.

A local wildlife guide named Emmanuel stood beside him.

“Beautiful,” Cage whispered.

“Yes.”

“Ancient.”

“Yes.”

“Dangerous.”

“Very.”

“Treasure?”

“No.”

Cage nodded.

“Good. Just checking.”

Then something behind them said:

“Ruh-roh.”

Cage turned slowly.

Scooby-Doo stood twenty feet away.

Beside him were Shaggy, Velma, Daphne, and Fred.

The Mystery Machine was parked beneath a tree.

Nicolas Cage stared at them.

They stared at Nicolas Cage.

Cage looked at Emmanuel.

Emmanuel shrugged.

“I stopped asking questions about tourists several years ago.”

Fred approached.

“Mr. Cage?”

“Nicolas.”

“Nicolas, we’re investigating some strange activity around the falls.”

Cage’s face changed immediately.

“What kind of activity?”

Velma stepped forward.

“Several research stations have reported equipment disappearing overnight.”

Daphne added, “And people keep hearing this horrible roaring sound after dark.”

Shaggy swallowed.

“Like, not regular roaring either. Haunted roaring.”

Scooby nodded.

“Raunted.”

Cage looked toward the waterfall.

His documentary crew suddenly mattered considerably less.

“What does the roar sound like?”

Fred played a recording.

A deep, resonant:

WOOOOOOOAAAAAAHHHHHH.

Cage’s eyes narrowed.

“Play it again.”

Fred did.

WOOOOOOOAAAAAAHHHHHH.

Cage raised one finger.

“That is not a ghost.”

Velma smiled.

“Exactly.”

“It is something worse.”

Velma stopped smiling.

“What?”

Cage whispered:

“A stadium.”

Everyone stared at him.

Shaggy said, “Like… a football stadium?”

“Yes.”

Velma adjusted her glasses.

“That makes absolutely no sense.”

“I know.”

Cage smiled.

“That’s why it bothers me.”

—

They began investigating.

The first clue appeared beside a damaged research station.

Tracks.

Large ones.

Fred crouched.

“Hippo.”

Emmanuel shook his head.

“No.”

Everyone looked at him.

He pointed.

“A hippo’s tracks would come from the river and return toward water. These circle the building.”

Velma knelt.

“And the stride length changes.”

Cage crouched beside her.

“Someone made them.”

“Probably.”

“With fake hippopotamus feet.”

“Probably.”

Cage stood.

“Someone is impersonating a hippopotamus.”

Scooby gasped.

Shaggy said, “Like, finally, a sentence worse than haunted roaring.”

They followed the tracks toward the forest.

Eventually they found something even stranger.

A football.

Sitting perfectly upright on a kicking tee.

Fred picked it up.

“That’s weird.”

Cage slapped it out of his hands.

“DON’T.”

Fred jumped.

“What?!”

Cage pointed at the football.

“It was placed.”

“Yes.”

“Centered.”

“Yes.”

“Inviting us to interact with it.”

Velma said, “It’s probably just a clue.”

Cage looked genuinely disappointed in her.

“Velma.”

“Yes?”

“You are supposed to be the intelligent one.”

Scooby covered his mouth.

“Ruh.”

Velma folded her arms.

“What do you think it is?”

Cage circled the football.

“A message.”

“What message?”

Cage stared at it.

“Fourth down.”

Shaggy whispered to Scooby:

“He doesn’t know football, does he?”

“Rope.”

—

That night they camped near the waterfall.

Nobody slept particularly well because Emmanuel had casually mentioned that hippos sometimes traveled overland at night.

Shaggy immediately relocated his sleeping bag into the Mystery Machine.

Scooby joined him.

Cage stayed outside.

He sat beside the fire holding the mysterious football.

Velma joined him.

“You really think football matters?”

“Yes.”

“Why?”

“I have absolutely no evidence.”

Velma waited.

Cage continued.

“But someone wants us to think hippos are responsible.”

“Right.”

“And someone left a football.”

“Right.”

“And the roar sounds like a stadium.”

“Sort of.”

Cage looked toward the darkness.

“Therefore football is either important…”

He turned toward her.

“…or someone desperately wants us to believe football is important.”

Velma paused.

“That’s actually reasonable.”

“Thank you.”

“You sounded surprised.”

“I was.”

Then the forest exploded.

WOOOOOOOAAAAAAHHHHHH!

Shaggy screamed from inside the van.

Cage jumped to his feet.

Across the clearing appeared an enormous creature.

It looked roughly like a hippopotamus.

Except it stood unnaturally tall.

Its eyes glowed red.

Its mouth opened.

And from somewhere inside it came the sound of fifty thousand people shouting.

DEFENSE!

Cage stared.

The creature roared again.

DEFENSE! DEFENSE! DEFENSE!

Fred yelled:

“Run!”

Everyone ran.

Except Cage.

He remained completely still.

The monster charged.

Velma turned.

“NICOLAS!”

Cage watched it approach.

Twenty yards.

Fifteen.

Ten.

Then Cage dropped into a quarterback stance.

The creature hesitated.

Cage noticed.

“Oh.”

The creature shifted slightly left.

Cage smiled.

“Oh, you beautiful idiot.”

He shouted:

“BLUE FORTY-TWO!”

Everyone stopped running.

The creature stopped.

Cage continued:

“OMAHA!”

The creature twitched.

Emmanuel yelled:

“What is happening?”

“I DON’T KNOW!”

Cage pointed toward Fred.

“YOU! GO LONG!”

Fred looked around.

“ME?”

“YOU HAVE THE BODY TYPE!”

“I don’t—”

“GO!”

Fred ran.

The monster turned its head toward him.

Cage saw it.

“Velma!”

“What?!”

“Crossing route!”

“I DON’T PLAY FOOTBALL!”

“NEITHER DO I!”

Velma ran anyway.

The monster shifted again.

Cage laughed.

“It’s responding to formations!”

Daphne shouted:

“Why?!”

“I HAVEN’T GOTTEN THAT FAR!”

The creature charged.

Cage threw the football.

It sailed beautifully through the air.

Far more beautifully than anything Cage had done suggested it should.

Fred caught it.

Everyone stared.

Even Cage.

“Huh.”

The monster immediately abandoned Cage and pursued Fred.

Fred screamed.

“WHY DID YOU GIVE ME THE BALL?!”

Cage pointed.

“BECAUSE APPARENTLY IT UNDERSTANDS POSSESSION!”

Fred ran faster.

—

The chase ended exactly the way most Scooby-Doo chases end:

poorly,

then accidentally well.

Fred threw the football to Daphne.

Daphne lateraled to Velma.

Velma panicked and threw it to Shaggy.

Shaggy screamed and threw it directly upward.

Scooby caught it.

The monster stopped.

Scooby froze.

Everyone looked at Scooby.

Scooby looked at the monster.

Then the football.

Then the monster.

“Ruh.”

The monster charged.

Scooby ran at a speed suggesting a substantial portion of his normal limitations were voluntary.

He sprinted toward the waterfall.

Shaggy chased him.

“SCOOB!”

Scooby reached the rocky bank and slipped.

The football flew from his paws.

It struck the ground.

Bounced once.

Then disappeared into a narrow opening behind the waterfall.

The monster stopped.

Completely.

Cage arrived, panting.

He looked at the waterfall.

Then the creature.

Then the waterfall.

“The end zone.”

Velma reached him.

“What?”

“The football went behind the waterfall.”

“So?”

Cage pointed.

“It won’t follow.”

Fred arrived.

“There’s something back there.”

Cage smiled.

“There’s always something behind the waterfall.”

Emmanuel shook his head.

“No, usually there is rock.”

Cage looked at him.

“That is the kind of attitude that allows secret tunnels to flourish.”

—

Behind the waterfall was a cave.

Inside the cave was something nobody expected.

A miniature football field.

Not toy-sized.

About forty yards long.

Artificial turf.

Goal posts.

Portable lights.

Speakers.

Computers.

And crates.

Lots of crates.

Velma opened one.

Research equipment.

Daphne opened another.

Camera gear.

Fred examined the electronics.

“So someone’s been stealing equipment and bringing it here.”

Shaggy looked around.

“Like, why build a football field in a cave behind a waterfall?”

Cage walked slowly onto the turf.

“That…”

He whispered:

“…is the right question.”

Then a voice behind them said:

“Because nobody comes looking for a football team in the Congo Basin.”

They turned.

A man stepped from the shadows wearing safari gear and an extremely expensive watch.

Velma gasped.

“Dr. Percival Crane!”

Cage whispered to Fred:

“Who?”

“Wildlife-tech billionaire.”

“Oh.”

Crane smiled.

“I financed several research projects in the region.”

Velma said, “And stole your own equipment?”

“Borrowed.”

“For what?”

Crane spread his arms.

“My experiment.”

Lights came on.

The entire field illuminated.

Cage looked around.

Crane continued.

“Modern football is too predictable. Coaches analyze everything. Formations. tendencies. probabilities. I wanted to create a completely new playbook by training an AI on…”

He gestured dramatically toward the river.

“…hippopotamus movement.”

Silence.

Nicolas Cage stared at him.

Then at Velma.

Then at Crane.

“Say that again.”

Crane smiled.

“Hippos are extraordinarily agile for their size. Territorial. explosive. unpredictable. I built models of their movement and translated them into football strategy.”

Fred blinked.

“You built a football offense based on hippos?”

“Yes.”

Cage whispered:

“My God.”

Velma said:

“No. Do not encourage him.”

Crane continued excitedly.

“Imagine an offensive line behaving like a pod defending water access. Running backs using territorial displacement. Defensive pressure modeled on threat responses—”

Cage stepped forward.

“You have invented Hippopotamus Football.”

Crane looked delighted.

“Exactly!”

Velma grabbed Cage’s arm.

“Stop helping the villain brand it.”

“Sorry.”

Fred pointed toward the fake monster.

“And that?”

Crane sighed.

“I needed to keep people away.”

Velma walked over and yanked at its head.

The giant hippo mask came off.

Underneath was one of Crane’s employees wearing an elaborate mechanical suit.

The speakers embedded in its chest produced the stadium roar.

Shaggy nodded.

“Okay, yeah, that’s more normal.”

Everyone looked at him.

“What? For us it is.”

—

Unfortunately, Crane had one more problem.

The noise had attracted actual hippos.

Emmanuel heard them first.

His face changed.

“Everyone.”

Nobody listened.

“Everyone.”

Crane was still defending his research.

“The simulations indicate—”

“EVERYONE.”

They stopped.

Emmanuel pointed toward the cave entrance.

Several enormous silhouettes stood beyond the waterfall.

Real hippos.

Cage whispered:

“Oh.”

Emmanuel spoke very calmly.

“Nobody run.”

Shaggy whispered:

“I really want to run.”

“Do not.”

Scooby whispered:

“Re too.”

One hippo moved closer.

Crane looked at his computer.

“This is incredible.”

Velma hissed:

“Turn off the field!”

“But the data—”

Cage grabbed Crane by the shoulders.

“You modeled your entire system on these animals.”

“Yes.”

“And now the actual animals are here.”

“Yes.”

“Then perhaps…”

Cage leaned closer.

“…we should stop pretending the model is more important than the hippopotamus.”

Crane stared at him.

Cage stared back.

Then everyone slowly looked at the enormous real hippo approaching the artificial turf.

Crane shut everything down.

Lights off.

Speakers off.

Silence.

They backed away.

The hippo walked onto the field.

Stopped around midfield.

Sniffed the turf.

Then defecated on the fifty-yard line.

Everyone watched.

Cage nodded solemnly.

“There’s your peer review.”

Velma laughed despite herself.

Even Emmanuel cracked a smile.

—

By sunrise, authorities had arrived.

Crane was arrested.

The stolen research equipment was recovered.

The artificial football cave was dismantled.

And the Mystery Inc. gang prepared to leave.

Fred shook Cage’s hand.

“You weren’t bad out there.”

Cage nodded.

“I understand football now.”

Fred looked skeptical.

“What’s a nickel defense?”

Cage paused.

“A defense costing five cents.”

Fred walked away.

Velma shook her head.

“You understood one thing about football.”

“Yes.”

“What?”

Cage looked toward the river.

“That the ball changes what everyone pays attention to.”

Velma paused.

“That’s actually…”

“I know.”

He smiled.

Shaggy appeared carrying a plate of food.

“Like, anyone want breakfast?”

Scooby was already eating from it.

“Scooby!”

Cage watched them wrestle over a sandwich.

Then something clicked.

He looked at the football sitting beside the documentary equipment.

Then Scooby.

Then the sandwich.

Then toward the river.

Velma saw his expression.

“No.”

“What?”

“You just had another idea.”

“No.”

“Nicolas.”

Cage picked up the football.

“I’m simply wondering whether the same principle applies when the contested object is a sandwich.”

Shaggy hugged the plate.

“Stay away from my offense, man.”

Scooby growled protectively.

Cage slowly smiled.

“Interesting.”

Velma pointed toward his documentary crew.

“Go narrate the waterfall.”

Cage sighed.

“Fine.”

He walked toward the camera.

The director called action.

Cage turned toward the roaring falls, mist drifting around him.

He looked contemplatively into the distance.

“Here, beneath the ancient canopy…”

He paused.

A hippo surfaced behind him.

“…the river remembers.”

The hippo opened its mouth.

Cage glanced over his shoulder.

“…and apparently runs a 4–3 defense.”

“CUT!”

Cage nodded.

“Worth trying.”

The hidden little thread in that one is that the football, the mystery, and the ecology are all attention-and-response systems—but Cage only notices enough of that to be useful before immediately abusing the insight to investigate sandwich offense.